  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 113 PM CST Tue Nov 3 2026\n\nDual surface lows over northern and southern portions of the\nmidwest are in the process of merging over the vicinity of eastern\nIowa. After which, models agree that the singular circulation \nwill then migrate northward into northern Wisconsin, and \npossibly retrograde to some degree into central Minnesota. \nThe primary impact for the CWA is that this will maintain thick\ncloud cover and rain/drizzle chances through Wednesday across \nparts of the south central, and especially in the southern James\nRiver Valley of ND. For the rest of the forecast area to \ninclude western and north central North Dakota, skies will \ncontinue to clear as high pressure works into the area.\n\nOnce aforementioned surface low kicks off to the east Wednesday\nnight, another surface surface high pressure will gradually \nfilter in behind and pass through the state Thursday into \nThursday night. Simultaneously, a ridge currently over the \nRockies/High Plains will gradually slide eastward over the \nNorthern Plains. This will result in mostly dry conditions and \ntemperatures that remain above average through most of the week.\nThe exception being Thursday where stated high pressure will\nkeep conditions cooler (seasonable) along, north, and east of \nthe Missouri River.\n\nFor Friday through this weekend, models are in fair agreement\nthat flow aloft will become more northwesterly as the ridge\nflattens. While the latest NBM is currently dry; embedded\nshortwave energy could produce light precipitation when all is\nsaid and done. A transitory ridge could then bring the warmest\ntemperatures of the forecast period to the western half of the\nstate, with the NBM currently plotting some highs in the 60s on\nSunday. That said, overall trends to finish this week and enter\ninto early next week are for well above average temperatures \nthat are only slightly warmer than this week, but increasingly \nabove average as the calendar moves further into November.",
